\section*{Document Potential Challenges in Cross-Chain Rights Management}

These challenges have been systematically derived from the literature spanning 2020 to 2025, real-world bridge incidents, regulatory reports, and the gap analysis (G1--G7). They constitute the known and anticipated obstacles that this paper must explicitly recognize and address.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Potential Challenges --- Description and Severity}
{\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{llLl}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Challenge} & \textbf{Description \& Evidence (2023--2025)} & \textbf{Severity} \\
\midrule
C1 & XCM Weight \& Latency & The execution weight of XCM is non-deterministic across parachains; benchmarks from 2025 indicate a variance ranging from 300 milliseconds to 4 seconds for the same message type, depending on relay-chain congestion. & High \\
C2 & Bridge Risks & During 2024--2025, over \$2.1 billion was lost due to bridge exploits (Chainalysis, 2025); lightweight client verification remains costly or underdeveloped for certain blockchain networks. & Critical \\
C3 & Metadata vs. XCM Limits & The current XCM has practical payload limitations of approximately 2 to 4 KB per message; elaborate rights expressions, such as LCC metadata, collaborator lists, and geo-rules, readily surpass this threshold. & Medium--High \\
C4 & Time-Clock Heterogeneity & Different blockchain networks exhibit varying block times and guarantees of finality, such as Ethereum approximately 12 seconds, Solana around 400 milliseconds, and Polkadot between 6 to 12 seconds. & Medium \\
C5 & Royalty Split Atomicity & Currently, it is not feasible to send royalties to 5--20 collaborators across different chains within a single transaction without utilizing multi-step bridges. & Medium \\
C6 & Regulatory Divergence & The European Union (2026 MiCA), the United States (SEC proposals for 2025--2026), and China each have distinct approaches to the regulation of tokenized content rights; additionally, certain jurisdictions may categorize specific subscription tokens as securities. & Growing \\
C7 & Oracle Dependency & Subscription auto-renewal, price feeds, or geo-restrictions may require off-chain data such as fiat payment detection and GPS information. & Medium \\
C8 & Wallet Fragmentation & End users may maintain wallets across multiple blockchains, such as MetaMask, Polkadot.js, and Keplr; however, seamless cross-chain signing remains inadequate in 2025. & Medium \\
C9 & Coretime Cost Volatility & Agile Coretime (launched in 2025) introduced market-based pricing; fluctuations during periods of high demand can render dedicated parachains economically unviable. & Medium \\
C10 & Finality Rollbacks & Polkadot can, in theory, reverse blocks (BABE/GRANDPA); an occurrence that is exceedingly rare yet conceivable. & Low--Medium \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Potential Challenges --- Impact and Mitigation}
{\footnotesize
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lLL}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Likely Impact on This Project} & \textbf{Primary Mitigation Strategy (already planned)} \\
\midrule
C1 & Subscription renewals or PPV counters could be delayed or fail during peak load. & Over-reserve weight + scheduled locations (XCM v5); fallback to on-demand parachain if needed. \\
C2 & Permanent loss of rights tokens when moving to/from Ethereum or Cosmos. & Use only trust-minimized, battle-tested bridges (Snowbridge, Hyperbridge); keep core rights on Polkadot-native parachains; external chains treated as ``secondary distribution'' only. \\
C3 & Truncation or splitting of rights metadata $\rightarrow$ inconsistent policy across chains. & Split into mandatory core fields (on-chain) + optional extended metadata stored via on-chain hash + IPFS/SubQuery reference. \\
C4 & Subscription expiry or PPV time windows interpreted differently across chains. & Use absolute Unix timestamps + relay-chain block number as source of truth; all time-based logic references Polkadot time. \\
C5 & Delayed or failed collaborator payouts $\rightarrow$ legal/trust issues. & Two-phase design: (1) collect all royalties in a parachain treasury, (2) batch XCM payouts every N blocks with failure refund. \\
C6 & Future system blocking in major markets or mandatory KYC. & Optional ZK compliance layer (RO5) + modular policy flags that platforms can enable per jurisdiction. \\
C7 & Single-point compromise or latency. & Minimise: use only decentralized oracles, such as Chainlink or DIA, and design the system to maintain functionality in ``oracle-failure degrade mode'' by pausing renewals rather than revoking access. \\
C8 & High friction $\rightarrow$ low adoption. & Progressive WebApp + embedded account abstraction (via Polkadot's upcoming nomination pools + proxy accounts) to hide chain complexity from users. \\
C9 & Sudden cost increase for the rights-management parachain. & Initiate on-demand core time; upgrade to a dedicated slot only after demonstrating revenue. Design the system to operate on any Substrate chain, if required. \\
C10 & Subscription or ownership could be reversed in edge cases. & Accept the same risk level as holding DOT or any Polkadot asset; no higher risk than existing parachains. \\
\bottomrule
